From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a733: tcon-tv, kms: service TCON_TV1's vblank interrupt

[Recipe cut: regenerated: the hunks that touched the removed TCON debugfs
knobs are gone.]

MEASURED, 2026-09-26, the 6 GiB board, kernel 6.18.53 built from the series as it stood that day plus
drm-a733-de-position-the-primary-plane-on-a-black-background, a monitor
on DP-1 at 1920x1200@60, display manager and kmscon stopped so nothing
else held the card. With the plane fix the plane check passes, and the
documented pipeline

  gst-launch-1.0 filesrc ! qtdemux ! h264parse ! omxh264dec \
    ! videoconvert ! kmssink driver-name=sun60i-kms

still stops at preroll:

  gstkmssink.c:1872:gst_kms_sink_sync: drmWaitVBlank failed:
  Operation not supported (95)

EOPNOTSUPP is drm_wait_vblank_ioctl()'s answer on a device that never
called drm_vblank_init() (drm_wait_vblank_supported(), drm_vblank.c).
This tree did not, on purpose: TCON_TV1's interrupt had no handler, so
every flip event was completed by drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank().
The same gap blocks drm_dp_start_crc(), whose sampler loops on
drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank().

WHAT CHANGES

sun60i-tcon-tv.c requests the TCON's interrupt and services it;
sun60i-kms.c initialises vblank for its one CRTC and sends flip events
from it.

  register              address       bit  field           access
  TV_GINT0 (TCON_TV1)   0x05731004    30   TV_VB_INT_EN    R/W
                                      14   TV_VB_INT_FLAG  R/W0C

  - The line is requested at probe with IRQF_NO_AUTOEN and unmasked only
    between the TCON's program sequence and its disable, i.e. while its
    clocks run; the handler never reads a gated window. It is masked
    (disable_irq(), which waits out a running handler) after TV_GINT0 is
    written 0 and before the clocks go.
  - The handler reads TV_GINT0. Without bit 14 it returns IRQ_NONE (with
    a dev_dbg of the word). With it, it writes the word back with bit 14
    = 0 -- the enables stay, other flags written 1 are untouched (W0C),
    only the vblank flag clears -- and calls drm_crtc_handle_vblank() on
    the CRTC the KMS enable handed it.
  - .enable_vblank sets bit 30 and clears a stale bit 14 in the same write;
    .disable_vblank clears bit 30. Both refuse while the raster is not
    running. One spinlock covers enable, disable and the handler's ack,
    because all three read-modify-write one register from different CPUs.
  - sun60i_tcon_tv_program() still writes TV_GINT0 = 0 twice, unchanged:
    the enable is DRM's vblank reference count's to set, not the modeset's.
  - KMS: drm_vblank_init() before drm_mode_config_reset() when the TCON has
    an interrupt; drm_crtc_vblank_on() at the end of .atomic_enable, only
    once DE and TCON are both up; drm_crtc_vblank_off() at the start of
    .atomic_disable, while the TCON still runs.

THE EVENT PATH (sun60i-kms.c)

  - .atomic_flush (new): takes the new state's event; drm_crtc_vblank_get()
    == 0 -> drm_crtc_arm_vblank_event(), sent by drm_crtc_handle_vblank()
    from the TCON interrupt at the next vblank, which also drops the
    reference; otherwise drm_crtc_send_vblank_event() at once.
  - .atomic_disable: a commit that switches the CRTC off gets no
    .atomic_flush (DRM_PLANE_COMMIT_ACTIVE_ONLY), so its event is sent
    there.
  - drm_dev_has_vblank() is now true, so drm_atomic_helper_check_modeset()
    clears every crtc_state->no_vblank and drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank()
    in the _rpm tail finds nothing to do.
    drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_vblanks() now waits one real frame before
    the old framebuffer is released.
  - Fallback: a TCON node without `interrupts` gets a dev_warn at TCON
    probe and a drm_warn at bind, no drm_vblank_init(), and .atomic_flush
    sends each event at once -- the old behaviour, now only where there is
    no interrupt.

sun4i_tcon.c (sun4i_tcon_handler(), sun4i_tcon_enable_vblank()) and
sun4i_crtc.c (.atomic_flush, .atomic_disable, .enable_vblank) are the
model.

What the page flip still is NOT: the layer write lands the moment it is
committed, wherever the raster is (sun60i_de_kms_scanout()). The event now
waits for a vblank; the write does not. That line-window guard stays stage
3's. Comments in sun60i-de.c and sun60i-dp.c that said the interrupt had no
handler are corrected.

NO DEVICE-TREE CHANGE. The TCON_TV1 node has carried
`interrupts = <GIC_SPI 58 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>` since
arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window; nothing read it until
now.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM -- the A733 user manual V1.00, each read on the page:

  - TV_GINT0 at TCON_TV + 0x0004, 15.2.10.2, printed page 722-723 (PDF
    743-744): bit 30 TV_VB_INT_EN enables an interrupt as each field
    finishes; bit 14 TV_VB_INT_FLAG is R/W0C, set during the vertical
    blanking period of every frame and cleared by writing 0. Line enables [28:25] /
    flags [12:9] and PSR bit 24 / bit 8 stay 0; 31, 29, [23:15], 13 and [7:0] are reserved.
  - 15.2.7.2 Module Configuration, printed page 711 (PDF 732), step 5: setting
    up the line/field interrupts in TV_GINT0_REG and TV_GINT1_REG is
    listed there as an optional step.
  - Interrupt number 90, source "TV1", vector 0x0168: GIC chapter table,
    printed page 594 (PDF 615); that is intid 90, gic_spi 58.
  - TCON_TV1 base 0x05731000: the node's reg, as every earlier TCON patch.

WHAT THE VENDOR DOES DIFFERENTLY (allwinner-bsp, branch cubie-aiot-v1.4.8,
drivers/drm/, read for this patch; no code taken). Its bit map agrees
-- flags [15:0], enables [31:16], LCD_IRQ_TCON1_VBLK = 14
(tcon_tv_reg.h:35-41, include.h:237-246) -- and so does its ack, a
read-modify-write that clears only that flag (tcon_tv_irq_query(),
tcon_tv_reg.c:65-76). Where it differs:

  - tcon_tv_close() disables LCD_IRQ_TCON0_VBLK = bit 31, reserved on this
    TCON, where tcon_tv_open() enabled bit 30 (tcon_tv_reg.c:98-111): the
    enable survives the close.
  - tcon_tv_open() enables the interrupt on every modeset
    (tcon_tv_reg.c:101), and its CRTC takes a vblank reference per flip
    that it never drops (sunxi_drm_crtc.c:2035): the interrupt never goes
    off. Here the enable follows DRM's reference count.
  - Its handler calls the CRTC's, and drm_crtc_handle_vblank() with it, on
    every interrupt; the flag only feeds a counter (sunxi_tcon.c:437-470,
    sunxi_drm_crtc.c:1600-1635). Here no flag, no vblank, IRQ_NONE.
  - Enable, disable and ack are unlocked read-modify-writes of the same
    register from two contexts (tcon_tv_reg.c:51-76 and :573-579,
    sunxi_tcon.c:952-966). Here one spinlock.
  - Enabling keeps a stale flag, so the first interrupt can fire mid-frame
    and count as a vblank. Here the enabling write clears it. ⬜ That the
    flag latches with the enable off is the UM's "every frame" read
    literally, not a register reading.
  - The handler is requested after the enable is set and freed before it
    is cleared (sunxi_tcon.c:811-834). Here it exists for the device's
    lifetime and is unmasked only while the TCON is clocked.

VERIFIED BEFORE THE BOARD

  - apply-patches: every series entry at --fuzz=0 on a freshly unpacked
    6.18.53, including arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-behind-iommu1, which
    edits comments in sun60i-de.c and sun60i-kms.c after this patch.
  - compile-check green; drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ rebuilt with W=1: no
    warning; checkpatch --strict: no error or check on the code.
  - a full kernel build (localversion -t390), for the test boot below.
  - a small test program (a733-vblank-rate), the instrument for item 1, built
    freestanding for aarch64 and run under qemu-aarch64-static: no card
    with a *kms driver -> exit 2; a non-DRM node -> ioctl errno 25, exit 4;
    a missing node -> errno 2, exit 3. Its ioctl number and union layout
    are static-asserted against include/uapi/drm/drm.h (0xc018643a, 24
    bytes, reply sequence/tval_sec/tval_usec at 4/8/16).

HARDWARE-PROVEN on the 6 GiB board, 2026-09-26 (6.18.53-t390),
1920x1200 mode:
  * a733-vblank-rate: 300 waits, sequence 691..990, steps != 1: 0,
    59.946 Hz, gaps 16525..16847 us.
  * 5731000.lcd-controller +300 over the run, +309 in the 7 s after it
    (the core's 5 s vblank-off delay); TV_GINT0 0x40000000 while a waiter
    holds vblank, 0x00004000 after -- the flag latches with the enable
    off, which settles the open point above.
  * drm_vblank_event tracepoints: seq counts up by one per event;
    drm_vblank_event_delivered carries a real sequence, none with 0.
  * kmssink's drmWaitVBlank, EOPNOTSUPP before this patch, now returns
    and the pipeline runs to EOS (see the plane patch).
  * X and kmscon unchanged, no vblank or flip_done timeout in the log.

OWED BY THE BOARD (the plan before the run), with the falsifier of each
(1920x1200 CVT-RB: 154 MHz /
(2080 x 1235) = 59.950 Hz, 16.68 ms)

  1. a733-vblank-rate prints rate 59.95 Hz, steps != 1: 0, gaps near
     16680 us. Falsified by errno 95 (no drm_vblank_init(): look for the
     "no interrupt in DT" warning), errno 16 (no interrupt within 3 s),
     a rate that is a multiple of 59.95 or far above it (the flag
     re-asserting within the blanking), or steps != 1.
  2. /proc/interrupts, the 5731000.lcd-controller line: read, run item 1,
     read again at once -- delta about 300 (more if X or kmscon also
     wait), i.e. 60/s while a waiter holds vblank; about 5 s after the
     last waiter it stops (vblank_offdelay).
     TV_GINT0 (busybox devmem 0x05731004) holds bit 30 during the run and
     has it clear some seconds after; whether bit 14 is set then answers
     the open point above (the flag latching with the enable off).
     Falsified by no line, a delta in the thousands, or "nobody cared" in
     dmesg.
  3. kmssink 1080p on the 1200 mode runs to EOS with no drmWaitVBlank
     error, and the earlier plane checks still hold.
  4. X (LightDM) and kmscon come up and behave as before; console proof
     16/16.
  5. Flips no longer complete by fake vblank: with the tracepoints
     drm/drm_vblank_event and drm/drm_vblank_event_delivered enabled
     (FTRACE is pinned), every delivery during X, kmscon or kmssink flips
     carries the seq of the drm_vblank_event just before it, at the same
     timestamp. A fake-vblank delivery carries seq 0 and has no
     drm_vblank_event beside it.

Assisted-by: Claude
---

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
@@ -1555,8 +1555,9 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(sun60i_de_kms_disable);
 /*
  * A new scanout address, staged through the same commit path as everything
  * else. This is the page flip, and what it is NOT is a flip synchronised to
- * anything: nothing here waits for vblank, because nothing in this tree
- * services the TCON interrupt yet.
+ * anything: the write lands the moment it is made. The TCON's vblank
+ * interrupt is serviced now, but it only decides when the flip's event is
+ * sent (the next vblank, sun60i-kms.c), not when this write happens.
  *
  * ⚠ AND THE MISSING GUARD HAS A SHAPE, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN "expect
  * tearing". The vendor does not write the datapath at an arbitrary point in the
@@ -1567,8 +1568,8 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(sun60i_de_kms_disable);
  * an update past it (sunxi_tcon_calc_judge_line(), sunxi_tcon.c:121-150). Two
  * independent line-window guards on the same write this function does
  * unguarded. So the risk here is not only a torn line: it is a write that lands
- * on a frame boundary. Stage 3 owns it, it needs the TCON interrupt first, and
- * the two functions above are what it should look like.
+ * on a frame boundary. Stage 3 owns it, the TCON interrupt it needed is
+ * wired, and the two functions above are what it should look like.
  *
  * A plane that MOVES is the same write plus pipe 0's window, and it carries the
  * same missing guard: for the part of one frame between the layer's size words
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
@@ -26,9 +26,10 @@
  *      on the screen; the same pattern read back and compared with cmp matches.
  *      Falsified by: no /dev/fb0; a cmp mismatch; or a screen that does not
  *      change when the buffer changes.
- *   ⬜ VBLANK IS NOT WIRED. TCON_TV1's interrupt (INTID 90 = GIC SPI 58) is in
- *      the device tree and nothing requests it, so drm_vblank_init() is
- *      deliberately NOT called -- see the block above sun60i_kms_bind().
+ *   ⬜ VBLANK IS WIRED AND NOT YET PROVEN. TCON_TV1's interrupt (INTID 90 =
+ *      GIC SPI 58) is requested by the TCON driver, which hands each vblank
+ *      to this CRTC; drm_vblank_init() is called when it has one, and flips
+ *      complete from that interrupt -- see the block above sun60i_kms_bind().
  *
  * ⚠ THE SINGLE LOAD-BEARING LINE IN THIS FILE IS `.fb_create =
  * drm_gem_fb_create`, NOT drm_gem_fb_create_with_dirty. That choice IS the
@@ -85,9 +86,11 @@
  *     call it at all without a Makefile move and an export on top of the ops
  *     refactor.
  *   - What is mirrored is 60 lines, not 256: the parts of sun4i_crtc.c that are
- *     vblank event handling for hardware whose vblank is wired, colour
- *     correction for the composite TV encoder, and the of_graph port lookup for
- *     a multi-pipe topology are all absent here on purpose.
+ *     colour correction for the composite TV encoder and the of_graph port
+ *     lookup for a multi-pipe topology are absent here on purpose. Its vblank
+ *     half -- .enable_vblank/.disable_vblank, vblank on/off around the
+ *     raster, the event armed in .atomic_flush -- came later, with the TCON
+ *     interrupt it needs.
  *
  *   So the refactor is the right long-term answer and it belongs in the same
  *   patch as the mixer move, where it is one change instead of two.
@@ -130,7 +133,7 @@
  *   - THE VBLANK INTERRUPT IS THE TCON'S. devm_request_irq() on the TCON device
  *     (sunxi_tcon.c:472-484), tail-calling the CRTC's handler; the DE's own
  *     interrupt is CRC-only (sunxi_de.c:1125-1138). That is INTID 90 = GIC SPI
- *     58 on our TCON_TV1 node, and it is stage 3's.
+ *     58 on our TCON_TV1 node, and sun60i-tcon-tv.c requests it the same way.
  *
  * TAUGHT -- three hardware rules that are in no register table, each with what
  * it means here:
@@ -188,11 +191,13 @@
  *     take crtc_state->adjusted_mode |
  *   | sunxi_de_enable()'s failure is logged and then ignored -- scrtc->enabled
  *     = true and drm_crtc_vblank_on() run anyway (crtc.c:1955-1959) | the CRTC
- *     enable stops, and the engine is put back down if the TCON then fails |
+ *     enable stops, and the engine is put back down if the TCON then fails;
+ *     vblank is switched on only for a raster that runs |
  *   | drm_crtc_vblank_get() in .atomic_begin (crtc.c:2035) with no
  *     drm_crtc_vblank_put() anywhere in the tree: after the first page flip the
- *     vblank interrupt is pinned on and .disable_vblank is dead | no vblank
- *     claimed at all until it is serviced -- see sun60i_kms_bind() |
+ *     vblank interrupt is pinned on and .disable_vblank is dead | one reference
+ *     per event, taken in .atomic_flush and dropped by the DRM core when it
+ *     sends the event (drm_crtc_arm_vblank_event()) -- mainline's pattern |
  *   | no plane .atomic_check of any kind (crtc.c:1082-1092): no clipping, no
  *     scaling bound, no visibility, and one of its two hooks needs an
  *     out-of-tree change to struct drm_plane_helper_funcs | the plane refuses
@@ -259,6 +264,7 @@
 #include <drm/drm_plane.h>
 #include <drm/drm_probe_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_rect.h>
+#include <drm/drm_vblank.h>
 
 #include "sun60i-kms.h"
 
@@ -686,18 +692,36 @@ static void sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_enable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 	}
 
 	ret = sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_enable(kms->tcon, &crtc_state->adjusted_mode,
-					sun60i_kms_crtc_bpc(kms, state));
+					sun60i_kms_crtc_bpc(kms, state), crtc);
 	if (ret) {
 		drm_err(crtc->dev, "TCON_TV1: %d\n", ret);
 		sun60i_de_kms_disable(kms->de);
+		return;
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Only for a raster that runs. On either failure above vblank stays
+	 * off, drm_crtc_vblank_get() refuses, and .atomic_flush sends the event
+	 * at once instead of arming it for an interrupt that will not come.
+	 */
+	if (drm_dev_has_vblank(crtc->dev))
+		drm_crtc_vblank_on(crtc);
 }
 
 static void sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_disable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 					   struct drm_atomic_state *state)
 {
+	struct drm_crtc_state *crtc_state =
+		drm_atomic_get_new_crtc_state(state, crtc);
 	struct sun60i_kms *kms = crtc_to_kms(crtc);
 
+	/*
+	 * Vblank off while the TCON still runs, so .disable_vblank writes into
+	 * a clocked register; it also sends any event still armed.
+	 */
+	if (drm_dev_has_vblank(crtc->dev))
+		drm_crtc_vblank_off(crtc);
+
 	/*
 	 * Raster first, then the fetch. The engine must not be reading a buffer
 	 * the atomic helpers are about to hand back while a TCON is still
@@ -706,25 +730,79 @@ static void sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_disable(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
 	 */
 	sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_disable(kms->tcon);
 	sun60i_de_kms_disable(kms->de);
+
+	/*
+	 * A commit that switches the CRTC off gets no .atomic_flush
+	 * (DRM_PLANE_COMMIT_ACTIVE_ONLY), and no vblank will come to send its
+	 * event, so it goes here -- sun4i_crtc_atomic_disable() does the same.
+	 */
+	if (crtc_state->event && !crtc_state->active) {
+		spin_lock_irq(&crtc->dev->event_lock);
+		drm_crtc_send_vblank_event(crtc, crtc_state->event);
+		spin_unlock_irq(&crtc->dev->event_lock);
+		crtc_state->event = NULL;
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * The event of a commit on a running CRTC. The plane writes above have landed
+ * -- they are direct stores -- so the event is armed for the NEXT vblank and
+ * sent by drm_crtc_handle_vblank() from the TCON's interrupt, which also drops
+ * the reference taken here. Without a vblank (no interrupt in DT, or a raster
+ * that failed to start) the get refuses and the event goes out at once, which
+ * is what drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank() used to do for every flip.
+ * sun4i_crtc_atomic_flush() is the model.
+ */
+static void sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_flush(struct drm_crtc *crtc,
+					 struct drm_atomic_state *state)
+{
+	struct drm_crtc_state *crtc_state =
+		drm_atomic_get_new_crtc_state(state, crtc);
+	struct drm_pending_vblank_event *event = crtc_state->event;
+
+	if (!event)
+		return;
+
+	crtc_state->event = NULL;
+
+	spin_lock_irq(&crtc->dev->event_lock);
+	if (drm_crtc_vblank_get(crtc) == 0)
+		drm_crtc_arm_vblank_event(crtc, event);
+	else
+		drm_crtc_send_vblank_event(crtc, event);
+	spin_unlock_irq(&crtc->dev->event_lock);
 }
 
 static const struct drm_crtc_helper_funcs sun60i_kms_crtc_helper_funcs = {
 	.mode_valid	= sun60i_kms_crtc_mode_valid,
 	.atomic_check	= sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_check,
+	.atomic_flush	= sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_flush,
 	.atomic_enable	= sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_enable,
 	.atomic_disable	= sun60i_kms_crtc_atomic_disable,
 };
 
 /*
- * No .enable_vblank / .disable_vblank, and no drm_vblank_init() below. That is
- * a stage boundary, not an oversight -- see the block above sun60i_kms_bind().
+ * The TCON's TV_VB_INT_EN, switched by DRM's vblank reference count: on for
+ * the first waiter, off vblank_offdelay (5 s by default) after the last.
  */
+static int sun60i_kms_crtc_enable_vblank(struct drm_crtc *crtc)
+{
+	return sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_enable_vblank(crtc_to_kms(crtc)->tcon);
+}
+
+static void sun60i_kms_crtc_disable_vblank(struct drm_crtc *crtc)
+{
+	sun60i_tcon_tv_kms_disable_vblank(crtc_to_kms(crtc)->tcon);
+}
+
 static const struct drm_crtc_funcs sun60i_kms_crtc_funcs = {
 	.reset			= drm_atomic_helper_crtc_reset,
 	.set_config		= drm_atomic_helper_set_config,
 	.page_flip		= drm_atomic_helper_page_flip,
 	.atomic_duplicate_state	= drm_atomic_helper_crtc_duplicate_state,
 	.atomic_destroy_state	= drm_atomic_helper_crtc_destroy_state,
+	.enable_vblank		= sun60i_kms_crtc_enable_vblank,
+	.disable_vblank		= sun60i_kms_crtc_disable_vblank,
 };
 
 /*
@@ -751,8 +829,10 @@ static const struct drm_mode_config_helper_funcs sun60i_kms_mode_config_helpers
 	 * engine's channel and blender register windows do not accept a write
 	 * until three de_top words have been set, and those are written as part
 	 * of bringing the engine up. It also calls
-	 * drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank(), which is what completes a flip on a
-	 * CRTC with no vblank interrupt wired.
+	 * drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank(), which completed every flip before
+	 * the TCON interrupt was wired. It finds nothing to do now: with
+	 * drm_vblank_init() done crtc_state->no_vblank is false, and in every
+	 * case .atomic_flush or .atomic_disable has already taken the event.
 	 */
 	.atomic_commit_tail = drm_atomic_helper_commit_tail_rpm,
 };
@@ -976,24 +1056,33 @@ static int sun60i_kms_attach_components(struct sun60i_kms *kms,
 }
 
 /*
- * ⬜ WHY drm_vblank_init() IS NOT CALLED, and it is a decision with an
- * observable consequence rather than a missing line.
+ * ⬜ WHEN drm_vblank_init() IS CALLED, and what it changes -- wired, not yet
+ * proven on silicon.
+ *
+ * TCON_TV1's interrupt -- INTID 90 = GIC SPI 58, in the device tree since
+ * `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` -- is requested by sun60i-tcon-tv.c, which acks TV_VB_INT_FLAG and hands
+ * each vblank to this CRTC through drm_crtc_handle_vblank(). When the TCON has
+ * it, drm_vblank_init() is called for the one CRTC, drm_dev_has_vblank() is
+ * true and every crtc_state->no_vblank false. Then: a flip's event is armed in
+ * .atomic_flush and sent from the interrupt at the next vblank;
+ * DRM_IOCTL_WAIT_VBLANK answers (kmssink's drmWaitVBlank got EOPNOTSUPP without
+ * it); drm_atomic_helper_wait_for_vblanks() waits a frame before the old
+ * framebuffer is released; and drm_crtc_wait_one_vblank(), the loop under
+ * drm_dp_start_crc(), has something to wait on.
  *
- * TCON_TV1 has an interrupt -- INTID 90 = GIC SPI 58, in the device tree since
- * `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` -- and nothing in this tree requests it. `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` deliberately writes
